      "writtenOn": "2025-03-22T01:43:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the first iteration, `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs` is not initialized, so this `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs.value_or(inputTimestampUs)` returns `inputTimestampUs` and the expression `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs.value_or(inputTimestampUs) - inputTimestampUs)` evaluates to zero.\n\nFurther, at line 418,\n``` \nmLastFrameEndTimestampUs \u003d inputTimestampUs\n                + (capacity / sizeof(int16_t) * 1000000ll / channelCount / sampleRate);\n```\nthis expression determines the next frames start timestamp.\n\nNow as the second process call is made, at line 404, if the input queued pts is less than this `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs`, then a warning is logged and the input queued pts is update to the `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs`.\n\nOn the other hand, if the input queued timestamp far exceeds the `mLastFrameEndTimestampUs` then perhaps some gap was intentionally introduced by the client. So the output timestamps are also need to make a similar adjustment. This is handled by resetting anchor timestamp.",
